
{"id":"c6cbcd9f-9082-4b56-b013-f4d605174093","object":"chat.completion","created":1784994753,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. It's a chunk reduction schedule. We have items (list of strings), and required_failure_items (list of strings). We need to apply a reduction process: start at granularity 2. Try chunks from left to right, accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity. Return reduced items, attempt count, and one_minimal boolean (presumably whether only one minimal set remains? Actually \"one_minimal\" likely indicates whether the reduced items are minimal in the sense that no further reduction is possible? But need to interpret from description: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" Probably one_minimal is true if after reduction, the set is minimal (i.e., cannot be reduced further while preserving required failure items). The algorithm is deterministic.\n\nGiven payload: items and required_failure_items. We need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"attempts\": int, \"one_minimal\": bool, \"reduced_items\": list of strings}.\n\nLet's parse the algorithm details:\n\n- \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" So initial granularity g=2? Or maybe granularity meaning chunk size? Probably granularity = number of items per chunk? Or granularity = chunk size? Usually in chunk reduction, granularity is the number of consecutive items considered as a chunk. \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" So we divide the items into chunks of size g (maybe overlapping? Likely non-overlapping? \"Chunks from left to right\" suggests we consider contiguous blocks of size g, sliding? Or partition? Usually chunk reduction uses contiguous blocks of size granularity, and tries to remove each block, see if the remaining items still contain all required failure items. If removal succeeds, we keep that removal and restart at a slightly coarser granularity. \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That means after a successful removal, we increase g by some amount? \"Slightly coarser\" likely means increase by 1? But then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So when no removal is accepted at current g, we double g and continue. And stop when g=1 (singleton granularity). So we start at g=2. While g <= something? Actually we stop at singleton granularity, meaning when g becomes 1? But we start at 2, double to 4,8,... but also after successful removal we increase by 1? That seems inconsistent. Let's read again: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: We have a current granularity g. At each step, we try to remove chunks of size g (non-overlapping? Overlapping? Typically chunk reduction uses overlapping windows to try removal; but they say \"chunks from left to right\" maybe sequential non-overlapping chunks. \"Accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" So for each chunk (a contiguous block of g items), we consider removing it from the current set of items. If after removal, all required failure items are still present, we accept that removal, update items, and then restart with a slightly coarser granularity (g+1?). Then we continue. If no chunk removal is accepted at current g, we double g (g = g*2) and continue. We stop when g becomes 1 (singleton granularity). But note: if we double and get > number of items? Then we stop? Also \"singleton granularity\" means g=1? But we start at 2, so eventually if we double and get 1? Doubling never gives 1. Unless we also allow decreasing? Maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when we reach the point where we try to remove individual items (g=1) and no more removals are possible. But the algorithm says \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" That implies we double until we exceed some limit? Or maybe \"singleton granularity\" means that the items list has been reduced to only one item? Unclear.\n\nAlternative interpretation: There is another common algorithm: start with granularity = len(items)//2? No.\n\nLet's examine the given fixtures. They are simple: items list of 10 strings t0..t9, required_failure_items are two of them. Let's try to simulate to see expected outputs? Not given. But we can reason.\n\nSuppose items = [t0,t1,...,t9], required = [t0,t9]. Start g=2. Try chunks of size 2: [t0,t1], [t2,t3], [t4,t5], [t6,t7], [t8,t9]. Try removing each chunk. For [t0,t1]: after removal, required items remain? t0 is removed, so required_failure_items would not remain (t0 missing). So reject. Next chunk [t2,t3]: remove, required remain (t0 and t9 still present). Accept removal. So items become [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] (removed t2,t3). Then restart with slightly coarser granularity: g=3. Now items length 8. Try chunks of size 3: [t0,t1,t4], [t5,t6,t7], [t8,t9]? The last chunk may be smaller if not enough items. Usually chunk reduction uses non-overlapping chunks of exactly granularity, but if the last chunk is smaller, it's still a chunk. So try removal: [t0,t1,t4] remove: required t0 removed, reject. [t5,t6,t7] remove: required still there, accept. Items become [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9]. Restart with g=4. Items length 5. Chunks of size 4: [t0,t1,t4,t8], then [t9] as leftover. Try first chunk: removes t0? Yes, t0 removed, reject. Second chunk removes t9, reject (t9 removed). So no removal accepted. Then double granularity: g=8. Items length 5, chunk size 8 > number of items, so we might consider the entire set as one chunk? If chunk size > length, maybe we treat the whole set as a chunk? Then remove all: required removed, reject. So no removal accepted. Then double again: g=16 > 5, stop? \"Stop at singleton granularity\" - maybe we stop when g becomes 1? But we are doubling and never hitting 1. Or maybe after doubling, if g > len(items), we reduce g to len(items)? Or we stop when no removal possible and granularity is 1. So we need to interpret more accurately.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is standard \"chunk reduction\" from software testing (delta debugging). Typically you start with a granularity of 2 (binary split), and you try to remove each chunk (non-overlapping) and check if failure still occurs. If you find a removal that preserves failure, you continue with the reduced set and same granularity? Actually in delta debugging, you increase granularity if no reduction found, and decrease after success. The description here: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That contradicts typical delta debugging where you restart with same granularity or finer. But this is custom.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" \n\nI think \"singleton granularity\" means that the chunk size becomes 1 (i.e., individual items). So after doubling, if we go from g=2 to g=4 to g=8 etc., we stop when g > len(items) and we also try g=1 at some point. Possibly the progression: start g=2. If removal fails (no chunk removed), double g. When g becomes > len(items), we set g = len(items) or maybe we have a phase where we reduce g? Not clear.\n\nMaybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when we have reduced the set to a single item? But then attempts and one_minimal would indicate minimal set.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the algorithm is: We start with granularity = 2. While granularity > 1: (since we stop at singleton granularity) we try to remove chunks of that size. If we find a removable chunk, we remove it, and then set granularity = max(granularity-1, 1) or something? Actually \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests we increase granularity (coarser means larger chunks). So after success, we make granularity larger (maybe g+1). When no removal, we double g (which makes it even larger). But that would quickly become huge. If we start g=2, success: g=3, then maybe next success: g=4, etc. When no removal at g, we double: g=4, then g=8, etc. We stop when g becomes 1? But we never decrease. That seems odd.\n\nMaybe \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" means we go back to the original granularity but slightly coarser? Or we restart the whole process with a slightly coarser granularity (i.e., increase the initial granularity). For example, after a successful removal, we set initial granularity to current+1, and then reset the items list to the current reduced list, and then continue as if starting over? But then we also have the doubling when no removal. The phrase \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" might mean: when you cannot remove any chunk at current granularity, you double the granularity (i.e., try bigger chunks). When the granularity becomes equal to the number of items (i.e., the whole list is one chunk), you stop. Because singleton granularity could mean the chunk size equals the size of the list? That would be \"single chunk\". That might be plausible: you start at granularity 2, try removing chunks of that size. If none work, double to 4, etc. When granularity >= len(items), you try removing the whole list as one chunk; if that doesn't work, you stop (since no removal is possible). That would be \"singleton granularity\" meaning the entire set as one chunk. And you stop after trying that. But then the initial g=2, and after a successful removal you restart with slightly coarser granularity - that might mean after a removal, you set g = g+1 (or maybe g = min(g+1, len(items))) and then continue trying removals from the beginning of the reduced list. That makes sense: you try to remove larger chunks after a successful removal to see if you can remove even more. If no removal at current g, you double g to try even larger chunks. Eventually you try g=len(items) (singleton), and if that fails, you stop.\n\nLet's test with example: items 10, required [t0,t9]. Start g=2. Found removal of [t2,t3]. After removal, items = [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] (8 items). Then restart with slightly coarser: g=3. Now we try chunks of size 3 on reduced set. Find removal of [t5,t6,t7]? That removes t5,t6,t7, required remain, so accept. Items = [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] (5). Restart with g=4. Try chunks of size 4: first chunk [t0,t1,t4,t8] remove -> missing t0, reject. Second chunk [t9]? Actually size 4, but only 5 items, so chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t8] and [t9] (last chunk size 1). The last chunk is not size 4, so maybe we consider all contiguous blocks of exactly granularity? The description says \"Try chunks from left to right\" – likely non-overlapping chunks of exactly granularity, and if the last chunk is smaller, we still try it? But it says chunks, not necessarily of exact size? In delta debugging, chunks are of size n/granularity? Actually typical delta debugging uses granularity as number of chunks, not chunk size. But here it's chunk size. Let's assume we try contiguous blocks of exactly granularity, and if the items list length is not a multiple, the last chunk may be smaller; but \"chunks from left to right\" could be overlapping? I think non-overlapping is standard.\n\nGiven the description, I'll assume we consider consecutive non-overlapping blocks of current granularity size, starting from index 0, moving by granularity each step. If the last block is smaller than granularity, it is still considered as a chunk (with its actual size). That is typical.\n\nSo at g=4, items length 5: block1 [t0,t1,t4,t8] (size 4), block2 [t9] (size 1). Try block1: removal removes t0, reject. Try block2: removal removes t9, reject. So no removal at g=4. Then double granularity to g=8. Items length 5, block1 [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] (size 5) since g=8 but only 5 items, so the entire list is one chunk. Try removal: removes all, required removed, reject. So no removal. Then double to g=16, but now g > len(items)? We may stop because we already tried the whole list at g=8 (which is essentially the singleton chunk). After doubling to 16, the chunk size is larger than items, but the only possible chunk is the whole list; we already tried that. So we can stop.\n\nThus final reduced items = [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9]. Attempts: count of attempts. We need to count each time we try a removal? The description says \"attempt count\". Probably number of removal attempts (chunk checks) that were made. In my simulation: g=2: chunks = 5 attempts (first 5 chunks? Actually we try left to right until we find an acceptable removal? Or we try all chunks? The description: \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" It doesn't say to stop after first success? It says \"accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" That suggests we evaluate each chunk; if it passes, we accept that removal (and immediately restart). So we don't continue to other chunks after a successful removal. So we only try chunks until we find one that works, then we apply it. So attempts count would be the number of chunk evaluations before each removal, plus any evaluations that resulted in no removal? And also the attempts when no removal succeeds (we try all chunks? The description: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" implies that when no chunk removal is accepted, we double. So we must have tried all chunks at that granularity. So attempts count includes all chunk evaluations.\n\nLet's count: g=2: chunks: [t0,t1] fail (1 attempt), [t2,t3] succeed (2nd attempt). So 2 attempts, then removal. Then restart g=3: items now 8. Chunks: [t0,t1,t4] fail (3), [t5,t6,t7] succeed (4). So total 4 attempts so far. Then restart g=4: items 5. Chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t8] fail (5), [t9] fail (6). So 2 attempts at g=4, no success. Then double g=8: items 5. Only one chunk [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] fail (7). So total attempts = 7. Final items = [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9]. one_minimal: It should be true if the set cannot be reduced further (i.e., no chunk removal possible at any granularity greater than 1? Or specifically that the reduced set is minimal with respect to the required failure items). Since we have two required items at ends, any removal would remove one of them, so it is minimal. So one_minimal = True.\n\nBut is this the expected output? Hard to know. But we must implement something that matches spec.\n\nThe spec says: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So one_minimal is a boolean, probably indicating whether the result is minimal (i.e., no further reduction possible). Since the algorithm stops when no removal at granularity = singleton (meaning entire set), and we have attempted all granularities from 2 up to singleton, and no removal succeeded, then the set is one-minimal. But note: after the last doubling to g=8, we tried the whole set and it failed, so no further doubling. So one_minimal = True if at termination the set is such that removing any chunk (any contiguous block of size 1 to len-1) would remove a required failure item? But the algorithm only checks certain granularities. However, the task likely expects that the final set is minimal in the sense that no single chunk removal would preserve required failure items. Since we tried all chunk sizes in the sequence, and none succeeded, it should be minimal.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that takes payload with items and required_failure_items. It must not mutate case. Use Python standard lib only.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n\n1. Parse payload: items = case['items'] (list of strings), required = case['required_failure_items'] (list of strings). We'll work with a mutable list for items (copy).\n2. Initialize attempts = 0.\n3. Set granularity = 2.\n4. While True:\n   - Let n = len(items).\n   - If granularity > n: # we might treat this as trying the whole set? Or stop? According to earlier, we double until stop at singleton granularity. I think we should treat g > n as \"the whole set is one chunk\". So we consider chunks of size min(g, n) but only one chunk? Actually if g > n, the only contiguous block covering the whole set is the entire list. So try removing the entire list. This is the singletion chunk. After attempting, we break because no further doubling? I'll implement: while granularity <= n, we try chunks of exactly granularity (if n % granularity != 0, the last chunk is smaller). If granularity > n, we treat it as one chunk of size n. After processing that granularity (whether success or not), we decide next step.\n   But the description: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" I think \"singleton granularity\" means the granularity equals the number of items (single chunk). So we stop after attempting with g = len(items) (or after trying chunk of size n). So we should consider g from 2 up to n, but not beyond. We'll have a loop where we try current g. If no removal, we double g. If g becomes > n, we set g = n (since singleton granularity) and try once, then stop. Or we can just try g = n when g > n.\n   - But also after a successful removal, we restart with \"slightly coarser granularity\". That implies we increment g by 1 (or maybe a small increment). I'll implement as g = max(current_g + 1, 2). But it says \"slightly coarser\" which could be +1. So after each successful removal, set g = current_g + 1. Then continue from top of loop with same items (now reduced).\n   - If no removal at current g, then double g (g = g*2). Continue loop.\n\n   - When should loop terminate? When we have attempted with granularity such that it equals the current length of items (singleton) and no removal succeeded, and then we would double g to 2*len(items) > len(items) but we already tried the singleton. So after trying g = len(items) and failing, we can break. Alternatively, we can continue until g > len(items) and then after trying the singleton (or whole set) we break. I'll implement a flag to track if we have tried the singleton. Actually the algorithm may also stop when g > len(items) after doubling, but then we try the whole set as one chunk. After that, we stop regardless. So we can have a condition: while True:\n       - If g > len(items): set g = len(items) (singleton). Try once. Then break.\n       - else: try chunks.\n   But note that after a successful removal, we reset g to current_g+1. If that new g > len(items), then we are at singleton granularity? Possibly. In that case, we should try the whole set as one chunk, and then stop (since no further coarsening).\n\nLet's design more concretely:\n\n- items = list of strings\n- required_set = set(required_failure_items) for fast check.\n\nDefine helper: can_remove(chunk): if we remove that chunk (list of indices? Actually we need to check if after removing those items, all required remain. So we can compute new_items = items without those indices. Then check if required_set.issubset(new_items). But we don't actually need to compute all new_items; we can just check if any required item is in the chunk. If yes, removal would lose that required item, so reject. So condition: chunk contains any required item => reject; else accept. But careful: required items are strings, each item is a string. So we can check if any required string is in the