ually we already incremented attempts in loop? Better count inside.\n        items = items[:start] + items[start+g:]\n        # Restart with slightly coarser granularity\n        granularity = max(granularity + 1, 2)  # slightly coarser\n        return True\n    \n    while True:\n        n = len(items)\n        if granularity > n:\n            # attempt whole set as one chunk (singleton)\n            attempted_singleton = True\n            # try removal of entire list\n            chunk = items[:]\n            if not any(r in chunk for r in required):\n                # Accept removal (would remove everything) - but required would be lost, so impossible\n                # But handle anyway\n                items = []\n                attempts += 1\n                # After removal, granularity = granularity+1? but items empty, break.\n                break\n            else:\n                attempts += 1\n                # No removal possible, stop\n                break\n        # normal progression\n        any_removed = False\n        i = 0\n        while i < n:\n            chunk = items[i:i+granularity]\n            # check if removal preserves required\n            if not any(r in chunk for r in required):\n                # we have a removal\n                attempts += 1\n                items = items[:i] + items[i+granularity:]\n                any_removed = True\n                granularity = granularity + 1  # slightly coarser\n                break  # restart from beginning with new items and new granularity\n            else:\n                attempts += 1\n                i += granularity\n        if not any_removed:\n            # no removal at this granularity, double\n            granularity = granularity * 2\n            # if granularity > n and n > 0, we will handle in next loop iteration or break.\n            # But if after doubling, granularity > n, we will attempt singleton in next iteration.\n            # However, if n is already small, we might want to break after attempting singleton.\n        # continue loop\n\nBut there's an issue: after a successful removal, we break out of inner while and restart the outer while, but we need to re-evaluate n. That's fine.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where after a removal, granularity becomes > n. Then on next outer iteration, we will go to the singleton branch. Good.\n\nEdge cases: If after removal items become empty? Possibly if we could remove all items but required would be lost, so impossible. But if required is empty? Not in fixtures. But we should handle: if required is empty, any removal is acceptable? The problem says \"required failure items remain\" – if there are none, then any removal preserves required? Actually \"remain\" means all required are still present. If required is empty, then condition is vacuously true. But not in fixtures. I'll ignore.\n\nNow test with example: Should produce attempts=7, reduced_items=[t0,t1,t4,t8,t9], one_minimal=True.\n\nBut we need to confirm that after doubling when no removal, we might double past the length and then try singleton. In the example, at g=4, no removal, double to g=8. Now n=5, g=8 > n, so in next iteration, we go to singleton branch: try whole list (size 5). It fails, so we break. attempts incremented for that attempt. So total attempts = 2 (g=2) + 2 (g=3) + 2 (g=4) + 1 (g=8 singleton) = 7. Correct.\n\nNow one_minimal: Should be True because no chunk removal can be done? The algorithm stopped because no removal at singleton. But does that guarantee minimality? The algorithm only tried certain granularities (2,3,4,8). It didn't try granularity 5,6,7. Could there be a chunk of size 5 that removes some items but preserves required? For example, remove [t1,t4,t8,t9]? That includes t9, so fails. Remove [t0,t1,t4]? includes t0. Remove [t1,t4,t8]? does not include required? Actually t0 and t9 are not in that chunk. Removing [t1,t4,t8] from [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] would leave [t0,t9]. That preserves required! So there is a chunk of size 3 that could be removed, and we did not try g=5,6,7. Wait, we did try g=3 earlier but at that time items were different. After we reduced to [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9], we tried g=4 and then doubled to g=8. We never tried g=3 again. But after a removal, we increase granularity by 1 (from 3 to 4). So we never go back to smaller granularities. That means the algorithm may not find all possible reductions. The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So it always increases. That implies that once we move to coarser granularities, we never try finer ones again. As a result, the final set may not be truly minimal; it's minimal with respect to the algorithm's progression. But the requirement says \"explicit one-minimal check\" – maybe we need to verify if the final set is minimal under the same algorithm when restarted from scratch? Or maybe one_minimal means that after the algorithm terminates, no further reduction is possible at any granularity (i.e., the set is globally minimal). However, the algorithm might not explore all granularities. So perhaps one_minimal should be computed by a final check: after algorithm stops, we try to see if any chunk (any size) can be removed while preserving required; if not, one_minimal=True. That would be more thorough. The word \"explicit one-minimal check\" suggests we perform an additional verification after the reduction to check if the result is minimal (i.e., cannot be further reduced). That check could be: try removing every possible contiguous chunk (any size from 1 to len-1) and see if any preserves required. If none, then one_minimal=True. That is a standard minimality check. Given that the algorithm may miss some reductions, the explicit check would catch that and set one_minimal=False if a reduction exists. In our example, there is a chunk of size 3 (t1,t4,t8) that can be removed, so one_minimal should be False. But does that make sense with the expected